+# The data-aware counterpart of .INIT_PLAIN: the rule .data_start() runs for a
+# family in no registry (see there for the calling convention). Only the two
+# ex-Gaussians have one. The rating families are not RT models, and their
+# constants were placed by measuring log-likelihood shortfalls across response
+# styles, which is a better argument than a moment could make.
+#' @keywords internal
+.DATA_INIT_PLAIN <- list(
+  cogmod_exgaussian = function(s, p) .exgaussian_start(s),
+  # shape = 1 is the ex-Gaussian exactly, and that is where the constant
+  # leaves it, so the same three moments place the same three parameters.
+  cogmod_geg = function(s, p) .exgaussian_start(s)
+)
+
+
+# Ex-Gaussian (mu, sigma, tau) from the first three central moments: the mean
+# is mu + tau, the variance sigma^2 + tau^2, and the third central moment
+# 2 tau^3 - the Gaussian contributes none - so the skewness pins tau on its
+# own and the other two follow (Lacouture & Cousineau, 2008). Declines when
+# the skewness is not positive, where the cube root has nothing to take.
+#
+# The skewness is capped at 1.7 before it is used, and the cap is the part
+# that makes the rule usable. sigma is the square root of var - tau^2, a
+# difference of two nearly equal numbers once the tail dominates, so a sample
+# skewness a little high starves the Gaussian stage - and a Gaussian started
+# too narrow is the expensive error, every response near the mode paying for
+# it, where a tail started too short is cheap. At the cap tau is 0.95 sd and
+# sigma 0.31 sd, which is as narrow as the rule will start anyone. Measured
+# over 300 samples of 200 trials at an ex-Gaussian of skewness 1.4: uncapped,
+# the worst start was 1.0 log units per observation short of the truth and 5%
+# of samples declined outright; capped at 1.7 the worst was 0.04, and the
+# median was unchanged. On real lexical-decision data (speed_acc) the sample
+# skewness sits at 1.8-2.3 for most participants, above an exponential's own
+# 2 for a third of them, so the uncapped rule declines for 11 of 17 and the
+# capped one starts every one of them 0.02-0.22 log units per observation
+# above the constants. 1.5 was a little worse on the very skewed truths and
+# 1.9 on the tail; the difference between them is small.
+#' @keywords internal
+.exgaussian_start <- function(s) {
+  if (!(s$skew > 0)) {
+    return(NULL)
+  }
+  tau <- (min(s$skew, 1.7) / 2)^(1 / 3) * s$sd
+  sigma2 <- s$var - tau^2
+  if (!(sigma2 > 0)) {
+    return(NULL)
+  }
+  list(mu = s$mean - tau, sigma = sqrt(sigma2), tau = tau)
+}
+
